ed States, at the meeting of the Christina River and the Delaware River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wiltshire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Wiltshire</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Wiltshire is a landlocked county of southern England, largely made up of the chalk downland of Salisbury Plain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilusa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wilusa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Wilusa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Wilusa was a land in north-western Anatolia named in Hittite texts of the late Bronze Age, appearing as Wilusiya in the record of the Assuwa coalition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wim-kok"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wim-kok.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Wim Kok</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Wim Kok was a Dutch trade union leader and Labour politician who served as Prime Minister of the Netherlands from 1994 to 2002.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/win-myint"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/win-myint.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Win Myint</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Win Myint is a Myanmar politician of the National League for Democracy who was president from March 2018 until the military coup of 1 February 2021.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/winchester"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/winchester.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Winchester</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Winchester is a cathedral city in Hampshire, England.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wind-of-change-speech"><span class="enc-name">Wind of Change speech</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Wind of Change speech was an address delivered by the British prime minister Harold Macmillan to the parliament of South Africa in Cape Town in 1960.</span></a></li></ul>
